From 6aa444bde7fc76ec6cd28d89cdde54e26e48c02d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 16:34:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] Finish the marketing redesign that landed only partially
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

main was left in a mixed state: the shared shell carried the new design
system, but the home page, the fonts and index.html did not come across.
The likely cause is a conflicted patch application where LandingPage.tsx
and index.html resolved to the older side.

What was wrong on main, all verified rather than inferred:

  * It did not typecheck. App.tsx passes onNavigateToPage to LandingPage,
    which the old LandingPage does not accept. `npm run build` is `vite
    build` with no typecheck, so this still deploys — it fails silently.
  * The marketing site was split in two. Home rendered the old navy
    ground (#0A2540) in Mulish, while Platform, Who It's For, About and
    Contact rendered the new near-black ground through the shared shell.
  * The three typefaces the shell asks for were never installed — no
    packages, no @import — so every heading on those four pages fell back
    to Georgia serif.
  * The four pages were unreachable from the home page. The old nav only
    holds anchors (features, pricing, security), so nothing linked to
    them.
  * index.html still carried the scaffolding title and pointed
    og:image/twitter:image at bolt.new's default, so shared links
    rendered another product's branding.

Restores the eight files that did not land: index.html, the font
packages and their config, the redesigned LandingPage, and the shell;
and drops LiveDashboardPreview, which the new hero replaces.

Nothing else on main is touched — the company project and regulator
settings work, its migrations and the dashboard change are all left
as-is.

Verified after the change: typecheck passes, build succeeds, 39 tests
pass, and home and Platform now render the same ground (#08131A) and the
same heading face.

