From b3a6877a836667538239d34ef0d2e57d495c9d7d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 17:28:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Port the landing page from the supplied design source
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Replaces the version built from Figma screenshots with a port of the
authored design file, value for value: type sizes, tracking, font-stretch,
the alternating section grounds, and the hero's gradient stack. Several
things the screenshots could not show are now in:

  * The real logo, both cuts — the on-dark lockup in the nav and footer,
    the light one inside the paper batch record. The drawn stand-in is
    gone.
  * The hero's motion. The batch record plays its assembly once on load:
    the rules draw, the results drop in, and two chips settle from their
    in-progress state to their final one — "2 stages open" becomes
    "Verified", "Stage 3/4" becomes "Stage 4/4". It is the product's own
    behaviour, so it runs once and stops, and reduced motion shows the
    finished state immediately.
  * The hero's grid rules and the lit horizon arc beneath the content.
  * Section bands that fade in and out of a seam colour at their edges
    rather than butting up hard, alternating green-cast and blue-cast.
  * The design's button order and weighting: the waitlist outline first,
    the demo fill second carrying the glow.

The full token set from the design source is added under its own names
alongside the existing ones, so a value can be traced back rather than
guessed at. Nav and footer move to the design's treatment in the shared
shell, so the other four pages inherit them.

One deliberate departure. The design's calls to action are anchors to a
closing section with no form behind them. The waitlist is real and writes
to Supabase, so that section keeps the design's two buttons and uses them
as the intent choice, with the email field directly beneath.

The fonts stay self-hosted rather than loaded from the Google Fonts CDN
the design file uses — the app's font-src 'self' CSP would block it.

Verified: typecheck, build, 39 tests, both logos loading, no horizontal
overflow at 390px, and all four other pages still rendering on the
updated shell.

